# What never needs uploading to Vercel.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Deploys are made with `vercel --prod --archive=tgz`, which tars the working
# directory. Without this file that included .next -- 6.3GB locally after a dev server and a
# production build -- producing a 1.7GB upload that Vercel answered with "Internal Server Error"
# and, before that, "Unexpected error. Please try again later." Neither message mentions size,
# which is what made it hard to see: the failure looked like a platform outage.
#
# Vercel builds from source. Nothing generated locally is wanted, and uploading it is not merely
# wasteful -- it is the difference between a deploy that works and one that fails opaquely.

# ⚠ EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN BELOW IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC.
#
# This file uses gitignore semantics: a bare `coverage` matches a directory of that name at ANY depth,
# not just the repository root. The first deploy that ever used this file failed because of it. Six
# real Next.js route directories are named `coverage` --
#
#   src/app/educator/analytics/competency/coverage
#   src/app/unit-manager/competency/coverage
#   src/app/unit-manager/workforce-management/{analytics,development,roster-governance,staffing-engine}/coverage
#
# -- and the bare pattern, written to exclude a test-coverage report, silently dropped all six from the
# upload. The build then compiled fine and died in type checking with six `TS2307: Cannot find module
# '.../coverage/page.js'` errors against a generated route validator, which names the symptom and not
# one word of the cause. Nothing in that output says "your ignore file deleted a route".
#
# The anchored form `/coverage` matches the root only. Only `coverage` collides today; the rest are
# anchored anyway, because `out`, `build` and `dist` are all plausible future route segment names and
# the next person to hit this would spend the same hour.
#
# NOT ANCHORED, deliberately: node_modules (nested ones are real), .git (worktrees carry their own),
# and the .env patterns -- a secret must never be uploaded from any depth.

# ⚠ THIS LIST WAS WRITTEN BY MEASURING, AFTER A VERSION WRITTEN FROM MEMORY FAILED TWICE.
#
# `du -sh ./* ./.[!.]*` at the repository root is the only way to write this file honestly. The first
# version listed what a Next.js project is "supposed" to generate -- .next, node_modules, coverage --
# and missed `.next-verify`, a 2.3GB local verification build that no mental model of "build output"
# happens to name. The archive stayed at 1.6GB and the upload died with "fetch failed", which reads as
# a network problem and is not one. `.next` does NOT match `.next-verify`: gitignore patterns match a
# whole path segment, so a near-miss name is a complete miss.
#
# Measured 2026-08-24: 9.7GB working directory, of which .next 6.3G, .next-verify 2.3G, node_modules
# 562M, .claude 427M, .git 51M and .vs 42M are all excluded below -- leaving roughly 65MB of actual
# source. If a deploy ever uploads more than about a hundred megabytes again, re-run the du and look
# for the new directory rather than assuming the list is still complete.

# Build output. Regenerated on every deploy.
/.next
/.next-verify
/out
/build
/dist
/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Dependencies. Vercel installs its own from the lockfile.
node_modules

# Version control and local tooling.
.git
/.vscode
/.idea
/.vs

# Local scratch.
/scratchpad

# A stale git worktree lives here -- a full duplicate of the tree, and nothing the build wants.
/.claude

# Local environment. Never upload a secret; Vercel holds its own env vars.
.env
.env.*

# Test and scratch output.
/coverage
/playwright-report
/test-results
*.log
